# Code generated by Claude
"""Frames a set of world points with the neck camera.

    targets, report = frame_points(model, data, points, cfg=FRAMING_CFG)

Solves the neck's joint targets so that the eagle camera looks at the centre of the points (the centre of their bounding
box) and ALL points sit inside the image with at least cfg["margin"] (10 %) of the image width / height to every edge. If
they cannot all fit, the best attempt is returned and the report says which points are outside and by how much (fractions
of the image size beyond the margin line); nothing is cropped silently. The result is collision-checked: the neck's
geoms (collision shapes) must be at least cfg["contact_margin"] from the other collidable geoms within 1.5 m of the neck base
(the rest of the robot and the scene's bodies; geoms of the world body, such as a fixed floor, are not checked). If the best
pose touches something, the next best collision-free one is used, and if there is none the best attempt is returned with its
contacts listed.

The answer must be repeatable, because the eagle camera is a policy input and the neck frames once per skill: the camera has more neck joints (shoulder lift,
elbow and wrist all pitch it) than the aim and the margin need, so many poses frame equally well. A weak posture term (cfg["posture_weight"], 0.003, towards
cfg["posture_pose"], by default the drive pose) picks one of them, move_weight is 0 so the start pose does not matter, and the refinement's initial simplex is wide.
Measured at the recorded handoff: 0.1 degree of noise on the arms moved the lift and elbow of the old solver by 1.0 rad and the camera by 9 cm; the new one moves
them by under 0.05 rad (3.6 mm) at about 0.5 degree of aim error. There is no randomness: the same points give the same answer, bit for bit.

`points`: {name: (x, y, z)} in the world (or a list of xyz). Only kinematics are used (scratch MjData, `data` is not
modified), so it can run while the simulation runs. `start` = a joint vector (default: the current neck pose) from which the
cost of moving is measured and from which one refinement always begins. local=True skips the coarse search and refines only
from `start` (cheap enough to run a few times per second).

Report keys: fits, worst_margin (smallest edge margin of any point, fraction of the image), aim_error_deg, camera_pos,
neck_targets, contacts, cost, points: {name: {px, py, u, v, depth, margin, inside, outside_by}} with the pixel position in
the image, the depth along the optical axis and the margin to the nearest edge (fraction of the image dimension; >= cfg["margin"]
means inside).
"""

import itertools

import mujoco
import numpy as np
from scipy.optimize import minimize

from src import robot_specs
from src.neck import neck_ids

